Issue 

 
The fundamental issue is whether law enforcement violated my constitutional rights under the Fourth Amendment to the U.S. Constitution and Article I, section 9, of the Oregon Constitution by stopping, detaining, and ultimately searching me without reasonable suspicion or probable cause. Evidence obtained from an illegal stop or search, including the magnet allegedly retrieved from your person, must be suppressed under the exclusionary rule. 

Legal Principles 

 
A warrantless search or seizure is presumptively invalid. The government bears the burden to establish its legality by demonstrating that the actions were supported by reasonable suspicion, probable cause, or a recognized exception to the warrant requirement. As the Oregon Supreme Court has consistently held, an officer’s subjective belief alone is insufficient; it must be objectively reasonable under the totality of the circumstances.  

Summary of Relevant Facts 

Direct Contact Between LPO and Deputies: 

On September 18, 2025, Loss Prevention Officer (LPO) Sarah Fillis, prior to my arrival at the store, decided to report the theft incident from the night before. Her call to law enforcement stated that she was reporting a “cold” theft and preparing for law enforcement evidence which identified the individual responsible for the theft the night prior. Officers left the Target location with what, as I understand from cursory review of our circuit’s findings, is evidence within their possession that has to be considered when tallying the totality of circumstances known to them at the time of stopping/detaining an individual. Ultimately, when loss prevention officer claimed AshLe was the individual in the footage officers had in their possession - this identification was conveyed to law enforcement without independent corroboration or investigation – officers plainly and blatantly ignored it.  

What’s more, instead of utilizing official dispatch channels and protocol, Fillis contacted Deputy Orozco directly, oddly bypassing critical oversight and creating ambiguity. Even if there is regular contact between loss prevention officers and law enforcement, this unorthodox “service call” should, at least, call the loss prevention officer’s actions into the scope of a matter of a question of law – and allow her credibility to be challenged. After all, the officer she contacted, immediately and initially, is not the same officer who had only but a few hours prior met with her to obtain the evidence from the prior night’s theft, casting even greater doubt on her intent as a loss prevention officer and her ability to fulfill her role ethically and accurately when conveying information. Oregon courts evaluate third-party tips with heightened scrutiny when they involve "informants," even if identifiable.  

Fillis reported that she witnessed a "security wire" being removed by me from a vacuum inside the store. There is no independent corroboration of this assertion in the record by law enforcement, nor does bodycam footage substantiate when or if this information was conveyed to deputies in real-time. The lack of temporal clarity weakens the State's argument of an imminent or ongoing crime and reduces the possibility that any reasonable suspicion is in fact, reasonable. 

Further, the officers’ reports obscure critical details about who observed specific actions and when, often referencing information passed along either by another officer or Sarah Fillis, the loss prevention officer. This appears to be deliberate and causes ambiguity and effectively blurs the lines, leaving unclear which observations were directly observed by the officers, which were relayed secondhand, and precisely when any critical event occurred.  

Without this alleged observation being notated in her near minute-by-minute narrative, no accusations of illegal activity remain supportable – which, from a reasonableness standard, would seem to completely nullify any claims of reasonable suspicion as well as probable cause. Quite literally, no unlawful behavior was directly observed by law enforcement nor loss prevention during the time spent “monitoring” us in the store (which is known as shopping while black and can be construed to amount to harassment, profiling and discrimination). This omission undeniably and critically undermines Fillis’s credibility, particularly when viewed against the backdrop of her profiling and misidentification of Ash Le’. Fillis’s highly questionable identification appears driven less by any solid evidence and more by a lingering resentment or bias after the previous night’s theft incident, which involved a Black woman escaping with nearly $1,000 of merchandise. 

If the facts are considered from the vantage point conveyed above, Sarah Fillis might well be identified more accurately as an unreliable informant, whose potentially biased and incomplete account does not withstand scrutiny under any test of reasonableness. Without any credible or lawful behavior observed, her accusations fail to meet the threshold necessary to justify law enforcement action, further complicating the matter of trustworthiness and highlighting systemic flaws in relying on such unverified accounts. 

The Supreme Court has held that, no circuit allows an officer to turn a blind eye to readily available information in determining probable cause. Officers must investigate objectively and consider all information available to them at the time – which would include exculpatory information of the true identity of the individual AshLe’ was profiled to be.  

When stopped, officers exhibited a clear show of authority after immediately arresting AshLe but had no reasonable suspicion in truth (absent the unrecorded allegation of the removal of a security tag) to run my identification to discover an outstanding warrant. Any subsequent arrest and search predicated on the warrant must still survive scrutiny of the initial stop’s constitutionality and Oregon courts apply a three-factor analysis to evaluate the reliability of third-party tips.  

While Fillis was identifiable, this alone does not confer reliability. 

Personal Knowledge and Detail: Also, the information provided lacked sufficient detail to verify its veracity with officers being told that the girl from the night before had returned. Courts require concrete observations, not speculative leaps or assumptions.  

Police Corroboration: There was no meaningful effort by law enforcement to verify Fillis's claims before initiating the stop – even with easily obtainable information within their possession. Such corroboration is a critical component of reasonable suspicion. 

Unlawful Stop Based on Speculative and Inaccurate Information: 
Reasonable suspicion requires more than an officer’s "hunch" or unsubstantiated reliance on third-party statements. Deputies must articulate specific, observable facts linking a suspect to criminal activity. Here, the officers relied solely on Fillis’s unverified and demonstrably incorrect statements, a practice rejected by Oregon courts. 

The Taint of an Unlawful Stop: 
Under State v. Benning, 273 Or App 183 (2015), evidence discovered during a search incident to arrest cannot cleanse the "taint" of an unlawful stop, even if a warrant exists. This principle recognizes that constitutional protections are not erased by fortuitous post-hoc discoveries. The magnet’s discovery was therefore fruit of the poisonous tree and should be suppressed. 

State’s Burden to Prove the Search’s Constitutionality: 
As no exigency justified bypassing traditional investigative channels, the State cannot meet its burden to show the stop and subsequent search were lawful. Lacking lawful justification, suppression is mandated. 

Conclusion: 
The deputies failed to meet the threshold requirements for reasonable suspicion, rendering the stop and ensuing search unconstitutional. The purported tip, devoid of corroboration or substantiation, falls well short of the objective reliability standard Oregon courts demand. All evidence derived from the stop, including the magnet, must therefore be suppressed to uphold the integrity of your constitutional rights. 

 
